## Issues

- [ ] `description` is 168 chars (under cap, fine).

- [ ] `intent` is 81 words (under cap, fine).

- [ ] `outcomes.desired` has 6 items (cap 4). Item 5 is the MCP-description
  drift meta-outcome and enumerates each issue tag (`naming-conflict`,
  `orphaned-research`, `missing-spec`, `orphaned-spec`, `broken-link`)
  plus the deterministic-vs-heuristic split. Forbidden content.
  Traces to: `outcomes.desired[5]` | Brevity Contract caps + Forbidden content (CLI tag enumeration, exact strings)
  Misalignment: spec-bloat
  Category: Brevity, Forbidden-content

- [ ] `outcomes.undesired` has 6 paragraph-length items including the MCP-description
  drift meta-rule. Compress to 3-4 ≤2 sentences.
  Traces to: `outcomes.undesired` | Brevity Contract caps + Forbidden content
  Misalignment: spec-bloat
  Category: Brevity, Forbidden-content

- [ ] `## Strategy` has 4 decisions (under cap) reasonable size, but mentions
  exact tag names. Mostly OK.

- [ ] `## Good examples` reproduces specific findings with exact path strings
  and exact "[naming-conflict]", "[broken-link]" tag prefixes. Forbidden
  content (exact strings).
  Traces to: `## Good examples` | Forbidden content
  Misalignment: spec-bloat
  Category: Forbidden-content

## Retro

aide_validate's bloat is the same pattern as several other tool specs: a
description-vs-handler-drift meta-outcome that enumerates the contract
surface verbatim. The right defense is a unit test on the registered MCP
description; the spec should commit to "issues are reported one per line
with a tag, path, and explanation; deterministic findings are errors,
heuristic findings are warnings" without listing the specific tags.
